scenarios.prompt:
I have this MobX action:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  this.items = items;
  this.loading = false;
}
```

What needs to change so it works correctly under MobX's enforceActions?

trialRecords.output:
Under `enforceActions`, only the mutations that happen **after an `await`** are the problem — they're outside the action's synchronous execution window, so MobX no longer knows they belong to the action and will throw `[MobX] Since strict-mode is enabled, changing (observed) observable values without using an action is not allowed`.

So `this.loading = true` before the `await` is fine as-is; `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` after it need wrapping.

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

With `runInAction` imported from `mobx`.

A few notes on getting this fully "correct" rather than just "not throwing":

- **Don't forget the failure path.** As written, if `getAll()` rejects, `loading` stays `true` forever. Wrap the await in `try/finally` (or `try/catch/finally`) and clear `loading` in the `finally`, still inside `runInAction`. Also set the error state there if the store has one.
- **Keep the public action thin.** If this is a UI-invoked public action, the project's convention (`rules/patterns.mdc`) is to make `fetchItems` do the guard check and delegate to a `private async` method that owns the real work and the `runInAction` block. Match whatever the sibling stores do.
- **`flow` is the alternative**, not a requirement. If the store has many sequential-await actions where repeated `runInAction` blocks are the dominant noise, `flow` (with `this.fetchItems = flow(function* (this: XyzStore) { ... })` in the constructor, since decorators don't apply to generators) collapses all the post-await mutations into the generator's own action context. For a store with one or two such actions, `runInAction` is the simpler and preferred shape.
- **`@action.bound` vs `@action`**: if React calls `fetchItems` directly (e.g. from an `onClick`), it needs `.bound` or it loses `this` — that's a separate bug from the `enforceActions` one but commonly found in the same method.
- **You can't use `@action` on the `await` boundary itself.** The decorator only covers the synchronous portion up to the first `await`; everything after is on you.

So the minimal fix is the `runInAction` wrapper above; the complete fix also handles the rejected-await path so `loading` isn't left stuck.

Under `enforceActions: "always"` (or `"observed"` with observers attached), the mutations after the `await` happen outside the action context, so `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` will throw. The `this.loading = true` before the `await` is fine — it still runs synchronously inside the action.

The fix is to wrap the post-`await` mutations in `runInAction`:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

If the project also wants the error path handled (recommended — otherwise `loading` stays stuck on failure), extend the same pattern:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.error = error;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  }
}
```

That said, this store's own convention (per the skill's async-action pattern) routes a public `fetchItems` declared as `@action.bound` through a `private async` method that does the real work, with every post-`await` mutation wrapped in `runInAction`. If you're matching that existing pattern, keep `fetchItems` thin and move the body into the private method rather than inlining `runInAction` into the public entry point.

Two alternatives worth knowing, but not the default here:

- `flow` — a generator-based async action from `mobx` where mutations on `yield` resume inside the action context automatically, so no `runInAction` is needed:

  ```ts
  fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemStore) {
    this.loading = true;
    try {
      const items = yield this.service.getAll();
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    } catch (error) {
      this.error = error;
      this.loading = false;
    }
  });
  ```

  Only reach for this if the store has many sequential-`await` actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks are genuinely the dominant noise — not for a one-off like this.

- Turning off `enforceActions` — not a fix. It silences the error but leaves the mutations untracked in a way that's fragile against future config changes and breaks the store's action boundary.

Whichever shape you pick, verify with the project's type-check and a test that exercises the success and failure paths, and if `fetchItems` is bound and called from the UI, confirm the calling component is wrapped in `observer` so `loading`/`items` changes actually re-render.

Under `enforceActions` (the default in most MobX setups is `"observed"`, so mutations of observed state must happen inside an action), the two assignments after the `await` — `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` — are not running inside an action anymore. When you `await`, the rest of the function continues in a new microtask, outside the action context that `@action` established for the synchronous prefix. That's exactly what `enforceActions` flags.

`this.loading = true` before the first `await` is fine — it's still synchronous within the action.

The fix is to wrap the post-`await` mutations in `runInAction`:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw error;
  }
}
```

Two points beyond the minimal wrap:

- I added the `try/catch`. The original leaves `loading` stuck at `true` if `getAll()` rejects, since the line that resets it never runs. Reset it in the catch (also inside `runInAction`) and rethrow so callers still see the failure.
- If this action is public/UI-invoked, keep it `@action.bound`. The store's `rules/patterns.mdc` async-action shape also suggests this public method stay thin and delegate to a `private async` worker that does the real work — worth aligning with if the file follows that convention, but the `runInAction` wrap is the part that makes it correct under `enforceActions`.

Alternative, if the store has many sequential-await actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks become the dominant noise: convert it to a `flow` generator, which keeps mutations inside action context across awaits automatically. For a single action like this, `runInAction` is simpler and I'd stick with it.

Under `enforceActions`, the mutations that happen *after* the `await` (`this.items = items; this.loading = false;`) run outside the synchronous extent of the action, so MobX no longer considers them part of `fetchItems` and will throw (or at minimum warn, depending on whether it's set to `"always"` or `"observed"`). The `this.loading = true` at the top is fine — it's synchronous and still inside the action — but the two lines after the `await` need to be wrapped in `runInAction`.

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

Or, if you prefer not to leave `loading` stuck `true` when the request rejects, put the whole post-await section in a `try/finally` with the `runInAction` covering the assignment and the `loading = false`:

```ts
import { runInAction } from "mobx";

async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
    });
  } finally {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
  }
}
```

Both mutations could also share a single `runInAction` if you clear `loading` only on success.

A few things worth checking alongside this:

- **The project's convention for async actions.** If this codebase follows the pattern in `rules/patterns.mdc`, the public action stays thin — a guard check, then a delegate to a `private async` method that does the real work and holds the `runInAction`. Match whatever the other stores in the repo do rather than mixing styles.
- **`flow` as an alternative.** If the store has several sequential-`await` actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks are the dominant noise, `flow` generators are the intended MobX tool. For one or two actions, `runInAction` is simpler and clearer.
- **An equality guard.** If `this.items` can be re-assigned to a value that's `===` to the current one and that assignment feeds a reaction feeding back into the store, add `if (items !== this.items)` before the assignment.

And confirm the component(s) reading `items`/`loading` are wrapped in `observer` — the `enforceActions` fix makes the mutation legal, but a missing `observer` wrapper is a separate and common reason a component still won't re-render afterward.

The two mutations right after the `await` (`this.items = items` and `this.loading = false`) run outside the action, because `@action` only wraps the synchronous portion of the method. Under `enforceActions: "always"` (or `"observed"` when the observable is observed), those assignments throw; under the default `"observed"` in some setups they can also fail to notify observers correctly.

There are two correct ways to fix it.

## Option 1: wrap the post-await mutations in `runInAction`

```ts
import { action, makeObservable, observable, runInAction } from "mobx";

class ItemStore {
  loading = false;
  items: Item[] = [];

  constructor(private readonly service: ItemService) {
    makeObservable(this, {
      loading: observable,
      items: observable,
      fetchItems: action,
    });
  }

  async fetchItems() {
    this.loading = true;                 // still sync, still inside the action
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;                // post-await mutations now inside an action
      this.loading = false;
    });
  }
}
```

This is the standard fix and the one `rules/patterns.mdc` prescribes. Note that `this.loading = true` before the `await` is fine as-is — it's synchronous, so it's still covered by the `@action` decorator.

## Option 2: use `flow` (only if the store is dominated by this pattern)

```ts
import { flow, makeObservable, observable } from "mobx";

class ItemStore {
  loading = false;
  items: Item[] = [];

  constructor(private readonly service: ItemService) {
    makeObservable(this, {
      loading: observable,
      items: observable,
      fetchItems: flow,
    });
  }

  fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemStore) {
    this.loading = true;
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;                  // mutations after a yield are fine inside flow
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

`flow` turns the generator into an action that resumes its mutations inside actions automatically, so you don't need `runInAction` blocks. Reach for this only when a store has many sequential-`await` actions and the repeated `runInAction` wrappers are genuinely the dominant noise — for a single action like yours, `runInAction` is the simpler and more conventional choice.

## Don't do this

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  this.items = items;      // throws / silently fails under enforceActions
  this.loading = false;
}
```

Just removing the `await` or switching the observable to a non-observed one would "fix" the symptom by defeating the point of the store, not the cause.

One more thing worth adding while you're in here: if `this.loading = false` should also run when `getAll()` rejects, wrap the body in `try/finally` (with `runInAction` still around the post-`await` writes), otherwise a rejected fetch leaves `loading` stuck at `true`.

The problem: `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` both run *after* the `await`, so they execute outside the `@action`'s synchronous body. Once the first `await` yields, control returns to the caller, and the continuation runs in a fresh microtask that is no longer inside the action. Under `enforceActions: 'observed'` (or `'always'`) that throws (`Uncaught Error: [MobX] Since strict-mode is enabled, changing (observed) observable values without using an action is not allowed`). Even with `enforceActions: 'never'`, mutations outside actions each produce their own reaction run and can't be batched.

Two fixes, both acceptable:

**Option A — `runInAction` (the default for a one- or two-await action):**

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

The `this.loading = true` before the await is fine as-is: it's still in the synchronous part of the action. Only post-`await` mutations need wrapping. If there's also a failure path, wrap the `catch` mutations the same way (or restructure — see below), so you don't leave `loading` stuck true:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
    });
  } finally {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
  }
}
```

**Option B — `flow` (only if this store has many sequential-await actions and repeated `runInAction` blocks dominate the file):**

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;
  } finally {
    this.loading = false;
  }
});
```

`flow` wraps the whole generator so every resume point is automatically an action — no manual `runInAction` needed at each step. Note it's a field arrow function (`fetchItems = flow(...)`), not an `async` method, and the generator needs its `this` typed explicitly. If this action is called from the UI and needs bound `this`, use `fetchItems = flow(...)` + `@action.bound` is not applicable here — `flow` already returns a bound-compatible arrow field, so prefer that shape and wrap any remaining public `async` orchestrator with `@action.bound` per the project's async-action pattern.

**What does *not* need to change:** the `@action` decorator stays (it's what covers the synchronous prefix, i.e. `this.loading = true`), and the method signature/call sites are unchanged.

Pick Option A unless the store already has several `flow`-style actions — don't mix both styles in one store without reason. After the change, confirm the store's `enforceActions` config value (it's in the `configure(...)` call at app entry or test setup) so you know whether a missed mutation would throw or only warn, and re-run type-check plus the store's tests.

Under `enforceActions` (or with `makeObservable` + strict mode), the two assignments **after** the `await` (`this.items = items` and `this.loading = false`) run outside the action's synchronous window — by the time the promise resolves, you're no longer inside the action, so those mutations will throw (or warn, depending on config). The `this.loading = true` before the await is fine because it's synchronous within the action.

## The fix

Wrap the post-await mutations in `runInAction`:

```ts
import { action, runInAction } from 'mobx';

async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

That's the minimal, correct change. The single `runInAction` block covers both post-await assignments.

## Things to also consider

**Error handling** — as written, if `this.service.getAll()` rejects, `this.loading` is stuck `true` forever. Add a `try/finally` (or `catch`) and put the flag reset in `runInAction` too:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (err) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
      this.error = err;
    });
  }
}
```

**`@action.bound` on the method** — if this is invoked from a UI handler and passed as a callback, make sure it's `@action.bound` (or an arrow property) so `this` stays the store. The wrapper itself still needs to be an action.

**The `flow` alternative** — if this store has many actions with the same sequential-await shape and the repeated `runInAction` blocks become the dominant noise in the file, `flow` (a generator-based async action) is the idiomatic MobX replacement:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: MyStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;
  } finally {
    this.loading = false;
  }
});
```

With `flow`, mutations after a `yield` are automatically treated as action mutations, so no `runInAction` is needed. But for a single action like yours, `runInAction` is the simpler and preferred fix — only reach for `flow` when the repetition is genuinely widespread.

## Why not just do this?

- **Not** mutating directly after the await and hoping `enforceActions` is off — that's exactly the fragility `enforceActions` is meant to catch, and it fails silently (no observer notification) in other configs.
- **Not** wrapping the *whole* method body in `runInAction` — `runInAction` doesn't await, so an outer `runInAction(async () => { ... await ... })` doesn't keep the awaited continuations inside the action; the async boundary still breaks it.

## Verification bar

Before calling it done: type-check passes, the post-await mutations are inside `runInAction` (or the method is a `flow` generator), and a test asserting `loading` toggles correctly on both the success and rejection paths still passes.

Under `enforceActions`, the two assignments *after* the `await` are the problem. Everything before an `await` runs synchronously within the action, so `this.loading = true` is fine — it still executes inside the action's transaction. But once you `await`, you've returned control to the caller, the action has already completed, and the subsequent `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` are now **bare mutations outside any action**. Depending on configuration that either throws (`enforceActions: "always"`) or warns (`"observed"`).

Wrap the post-await mutations in `runInAction`, and make sure the whole method is still marked as an action:

```ts
@action.bound
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw error;
  }
}
```

Key points:

1. **`runInAction` around post-await mutations** — this is the required change. Both `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` must move inside it. You can group them into a single `runInAction` block.

2. **Error path** — your original code has no `try/catch`, so if `getAll()` rejects, `this.loading` stays `true` forever. Handle the failure case (reset `loading`, and either rethrow or set an error field). Put that reset in its own `runInAction`.

3. **Keep the `@action` decorator** — it still matters for the synchronous prefix (`this.loading = true`) and to signal intent, even though `runInAction` covers the async tail.

An alternative if the repeated `try/catch` + `runInAction` noise becomes dominant across many sequential-await actions is to convert the method to a `flow` generator:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: XyzStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;
  } finally {
    this.loading = false;
  }
});
```

With `flow`, every `yield` boundary resumes inside an implicit action, so no `runInAction` is needed and the `finally` gives you the error-safe reset for free. Only reach for `flow` when the project's many async actions genuinely justify it — for a single method, `runInAction` is the simpler, more explicit fix.

One note against the "just move the assignments before the await" temptation: you can't, since `items` isn't available until the promise resolves. The mutation is inherently post-await, so it inherently needs `runInAction` (or `flow`).

The two assignments that happen **after** the `await` — `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` — are the problem. Under `enforceActions: "always"` (or `"observed"` with an observer reading those fields), any observable mutation outside an action throws. When `fetchItems()` resumes after `await this.service.getAll()`, it is no longer synchronously inside the action's execution, so those two assignments are outside action context.

`this.loading = true` is fine as-is: it runs synchronously in the same tick the action was invoked, so it's still inside the action.

Two fixes.

**Fix 1 — wrap the post-await mutations in `runInAction`:**

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = await this.service.getAll();
  runInAction(() => {
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  });
}
```

Note `runInAction` needs importing from `mobx`. This is the fix to default to.

**Fix 2 — convert to `flow`** (if this store has many sequential-await actions and repeating `runInAction` blocks is becoming the dominant noise):

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemsStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  const items = yield this.service.getAll();
  this.items = items;
  this.loading = false;
});
```

Inside a `flow` generator, `yield` points are automatically treated as action boundaries, so no wrapping is needed. It also gives you cancellation via `.cancel()`. The cost is the generator syntax and the `this` typing annotation.

**Also worth adding, regardless of which fix you use** — an error path. Right now if `getAll()` rejects, `loading` stays `true` forever. Either add a `try/finally`:

```ts
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
    });
  } finally {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
  }
}
```

...or catch the rejection and set an error field in the same `runInAction` block as clearing `loading`.

One more thing to check: if `getAll()` returns an array you assign to an `@observable` array field, and the field is typed as a plain `Item[]`, you may hit the array-typing friction covered by the project's observable-collection convention — but that's separate from the `enforceActions` issue above.

Under `enforceActions` (`configure({ enforceActions: 'observed' })` or `'always'`), the two mutations after the `await` — `this.items = items` and `this.loading = false` — run outside any action, so they'll throw. Only `this.loading = true` on the first line is fine, since it runs synchronously inside the action before the first `await`.

## What to change

Wrap every state mutation that happens after an `await` in `runInAction`:

```ts
@action
async fetchItems() {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = await this.service.getAll();
    runInAction(() => {
      this.items = items;
      this.loading = false;
    });
  } catch (e) {
    runInAction(() => {
      this.loading = false;
    });
    throw e;
  }
}
```

Three things are happening here:

1. **`runInAction` around the post-`await` mutations.** After `await`, the synchronous action context is gone, so `this.items = ...` and `this.loading = false` are no longer in an action. `runInAction` re-establishes one for the synchronous block.

2. **`try`/`catch` so `loading` resets on failure.** In the original, if `getAll()` rejects, the `await` throws out of the action and `this.loading = false` never runs — the store is stuck loading forever. The catch block resets it inside its own `runInAction` (it's a separate action context) and rethrows so the caller still sees the error.

3. **`@action` on the method.** Keep the annotation on the public async method — it covers the synchronous prefix (`this.loading = true`) and declares intent. The public method staying thin and delegating to a `private async` worker is the pattern `rules/patterns.mdc` describes; for a method this small, inlining is fine, but if the body grows, split it.

## The `flow` alternative

If the store has several sequential-await actions and the repeated `runInAction` blocks get noisy, reach for `flow` instead:

```ts
fetchItems = flow(function* (this: ItemsStore) {
  this.loading = true;
  try {
    const items = yield this.service.getAll();
    this.items = items;
    this.loading = false;
  } catch (e) {
    this.loading = false;
    throw e;
  }
});
```

`flow` wraps the generator so every `yield` resumes inside an action — no manual `runInAction` needed. Note that within a single generator, mutations after a `yield` are already in the action context, so the `catch` block there doesn't need its own wrapper. Use `flow` only when it genuinely reduces noise; don't mix both styles in one store.

## What *not* to do

- Don't just disable `enforceActions` to make it pass — that's the exact failure mode the setting exists to catch (untracked mutations that observers silently miss).
- Don't leave the bare `this.loading = false` in the `catch` outside any action; it has the same problem as the main body.

After the change, confirm the type-check passes, the action's mutations all sit in `runInAction` (or the method is a `flow`), and there's a test covering both the success path and the rejection path resetting `loading`.

scenarios.prompt:
This component reads `store.items.length` in its JSX but never updates when items change:

```tsx
function ItemList({ store }: { store: ItemStore }) {
  return <div>{store.items.length} items</div>;
}
```

What's wrong and how do I fix it?

trialRecords.output:
The component is missing the `observer` wrapper. MobX only re-renders a component when the component itself is tracked as an observer; a plain function component receives the store as a prop and will render once, then never again when `store.items` changes — React has no idea an observable mutation happened.

Fix: wrap the component in `observer` from `mobx-react-lite`.

```tsx
import { observer } from 'mobx-react-lite';

const ItemList = observer(function ItemList({ store }: { store: ItemStore }) {
  return <div>{store.items.length} items</div>;
});
```

That alone is the fix for this specific snippet: reading `store.items.length` inside an `observer` component establishes a dependency on both `items` and its `length`, so a change to either re-renders this component.

A few related things to check while you're in here, since they produce the same "doesn't re-render" symptom and often get mistaken for a missing `observer`:

- **Reading the value too early / breaking tracking.** If you later destructure or pull the observable out before the JSX (e.g. `const { items } = store` or `const items = store.items` at the top), you may read the value outside a tracked render context, or track the wrong thing. Read observables as late as possible, inside JSX or inside the `observer` body.
- **Missing `observer` on a child, not the parent.** If `ItemList` is fine but a component *it* renders reads the store, that child needs its own `observer` too — tracking is per-component.
- **State kept in `useState` instead of the store.** If `items` was mirrored into local `useState`, an `observer` wrapper won't help; the component is reading React state, not the observable.

If your store also exposes `items` via a `@computed get items()` getter, no change is needed — `observer` tracks computed values the same way.

One note on the "why won't it re-render" instinct: don't reach for a `key` prop or force a remount to chase this. Add the `observer` wrapper (and check the read-late/child-observer causes above) rather than papering over it — a forced remount will re-read the current value but still won't subscribe to future changes.

The component is missing the `observer` wrapper. Without it, React has no way to know that `store.items.length` is an observable it depends on, so it never subscribes to that observable and never re-renders when `items` changes. The component just renders once with whatever value it happened to read at mount.

Fix: wrap the component in `observer` from `mobx-react-lite`.

